WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:26.020
Borgerson: In some respects the Arctic is the least explored part of the world.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:33.020
We know more about the surface of the moon than we know about the Arctic.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.900
One thing that scientists universally agree on is that the Arctic

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:41.920
is warming. The ice is melting rapidly,

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.920
allowing all kinds of access to new resources and geographies that heretofore

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.440
had been frozen and locked away.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:53.850
And that rate and pace of change is having profound implications for the region and for the world.

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:57.710
Byers: When I think about the Arctic, I don't think about the Arctic in

00:00:57.710 --> 00:00:59.120
isolation. I think about it

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.440
in terms of its location in the center

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:05.530
of some major geopolitical developments,

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:09.220
the so-called 'reset' between the United States and Russia,

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:12.540
the crisis of global climate change,

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:16.020
the development of China as a major

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:19.210
economic actor who is dependent on

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:22.770
shipping to have access to resources and markets.

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:27.369
The Arctic is in the middle of all of this. Conley: Oil and gas resources,

00:01:27.369 --> 00:01:30.990
mineral resources, fisheries, tourism,

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:35.340
shipping, all of these factors are profoundly shaping

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:38.800
the new geo-economics of the Arctic. They could

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:43.819
be transformative for both the global economy, as far as shipping and mineral resources,

00:01:43.819 --> 00:01:47.200
but certainly a huge boost to the

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:50.410
Arctic states: Russia, Norway,

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:55.410
Denmark via Greenland, Canada, and the United States.

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:59.280
International law, and the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea,

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:03.620
gives those five coastal states the right to explore those resources

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:06.950
in their exclusive economic zone, or EEZ,

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:10.600
200 nautical miles from their coast. And if you can prove

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:14.160
that your outer continental shelf extends, you can

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:18.010
exploit and explore those resources on the seabed.

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:22.310
Harper: Canada's new government has put the north higher on the domestic policy

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:23.090
agenda

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:28.040
than it has been for half a century. All Canadians need to recognize there is a

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:29.680
convergence of economic,

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:33.820
environmental and strategic factorsenvironmental and strategic factors occurring here that will have critical

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:34.490
impact

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:38.820
on the future of our country. Conley: Should these important shipping routes

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:42.940
open, like the Northwest Passage, which goes through Canadian

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:47.100
territory, and the Northeast Passage or the Northern Sea Route, which goes

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:51.190
above Russia, it could cut off shipping times between

00:02:51.190 --> 00:02:54.400
East and West. And then, beyond the 200

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.680
nautical mile exclusive economic zone will be international waters.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:02.590
We call it the 'donut hole,' and it's that circle around the North Pole

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:07.240
that are the high seas. And so there are non-Arctic states like China,

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:10.860
other Asian countries, as far afield as India,

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:14.880
are saying, well, eventually we can explore those resources. Those are

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:21.880
international waters.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:26.350
Borgerson: The Arctic has a rapidly changing environment;

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:29.520
in some cases it has blurred borders; it has

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:32.690
very rich natural resources. And in the past

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:37.250
that could lead to increased competition and perhaps military friction.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:41.450
However, recent years show that Arctic nations are actually working

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:44.480
in a concerted way towards collaboration and cooperation

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:48.800
through organizations such as the Arctic Council, through bilateral agreements,

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:52.660
and through a whole host of multilateral regimes based upon

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:56.280
the solid bedrock of the UN Convention on the Law of the Sea.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:00.090
But it's something that the world should definitely keep an eye on.

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:04.030
In particular, how Russia chooses to behave

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:07.160
in the new Arctic neighborhood will be very important

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:10.670
in terms of how the Arctic develops.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:32.919
Laruelle: Russia's arctic strategy is largely driven by

00:04:32.919 --> 00:04:37.120
the need for Russia to be able to use arctic region as a base for its

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:41.360
energy needs in the future. On the international side, I think it's mostly

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:44.430
an issue of prestige and international recognition

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:47.800
of Russia as being one of the key,

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:51.640
if not the key, actor in the Arctic. Byers: The Arctic Council was established

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:55.760
as part of an effort to engage Russia in the post-Cold War

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:58.780
era, and the Arctic Council has grown into

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:02.210
a regional organization. It has stepped up to the plate

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:05.840
as the place where multilateralism takes place

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:09.480
in the Arctic.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:14.210
Conley: The Arctic isn't a crisis today. Even though you sometimes see

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:18.320
headlines that say 'the new Cold War' and 'the race for the resources,'

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:21.960
it's an area of cooperation. But we have to think

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:25.220
in the long term, and make difficult choices about

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:28.600
how we spend our precious resources,

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:31.669
and how we protect our precious

00:05:31.669 --> 00:05:36.530
environmental resources. Scaroni: We believe that the Arctic is a

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:39.810
new frontier for oil and gas. It's going to be

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:43.320
relatively expensive oil and gas, nothing cheap

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:46.729
over there. But if you think that the oil prices are going to be around

00:05:46.729 --> 00:05:50.900
$90, $100, it's still competitive. Oulahsen: It's a disaster waiting to

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:51.440
happen.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:55.270
We can see the Arctic sea-ice melting in front of our own eyes and instead of

00:05:55.270 --> 00:05:56.030
seeing that

00:05:56.030 --> 00:05:59.400
as a huge threat to mankind and the planet,

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:02.630
oil companies are rushing into the Arctic to drill for the

00:06:02.630 --> 00:06:09.630
oil that caused climate change in the first place. Byers: The Arctic is changing,

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:13.930
but it's not becoming safer. And to reduce those risks,

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:17.729
companies and countries will have to invest literally billions of dollars.

00:06:17.729 --> 00:06:20.770
Arctic governments

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:24.630
will have to decide whether they're prepared to take decisions together

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:28.950
that will raise the costs of Arctic development,

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:32.520
internalize some of the externalities, and perhaps

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:35.810
put something of an economic disincentive

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:39.840
on some of the excitement about economic development that we

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:43.610
currently hear.

00:06:45.169 --> 00:06:49.080
Borgerson: The United States, like the other Arctic nations, needs to make investments into

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:50.270
hard infrastructure:

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:54.349
airfields, roads, and ports and ships to operate there.

00:06:54.349 --> 00:06:58.399
We need to chart the Arctic. We need better research and bathymetric data.

00:06:58.399 --> 00:07:01.879
So this begins really with a change in attitude

00:07:01.879 --> 00:07:08.879
of the Arctic's increasing relevance and centrality to international relations.

